1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:02,040
A road that tunnels through the Rockies.

2
00:00:02,700 --> 00:00:06,140
An epic byway connecting two great national parks.

3
00:00:06,960 --> 00:00:09,460
A route that's beckoned a century of challenges.

4
00:00:10,460 --> 00:00:13,200
A mountain pass that takes an army to keep clear.

5
00:00:13,820 --> 00:00:17,180
And a road that reveals some of the most beautiful scenery in the world.

6
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:19,660
So come on along for the ride.

7
00:00:20,200 --> 00:00:23,220
Now, Mountain Roads on Modern Marvels.

8
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,500
In Northern California, Donner Pass traverses the Sierra Nevada mountain range.

9
00:00:43,501 --> 00:00:48,360
Infamous for a dark chapter in its history, this scenic path has long served as a vital

10
00:00:48,361 --> 00:00:54,840
artery, allowing men, machinery, goods and information to pass from the Great Plains

11
00:00:54,841 --> 00:00:57,760
to the Pacific Ocean.

12
00:00:57,761 --> 00:01:03,600
The historic passage is part of I-80, a transcontinental superhighway that connects New Jersey with California.

13
00:01:04,700 --> 00:01:11,080
The Donner section of I-80 plays a critical role by maintaining the flow of interstate commerce.

14
00:01:15,420 --> 00:01:20,480
And as with most mountain roads, building it was just the first of many challenges.

15
00:01:20,481 --> 00:01:30,200
Donner Pass, as formidable as it was for the Donner Party back in the 1800s, it can still be that deadly today.

16
00:01:32,140 --> 00:01:35,520
Each year, an average of 42 feet of snow falls here.

17
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:40,240
But severe storms can dump as much as 20 feet in a matter of days.

18
00:01:41,700 --> 00:01:45,000
Visiting motorists are often unaccustomed to the challenges of driving here.

19
00:01:45,900 --> 00:01:49,080
A family vacation can quickly turn into a life-threatening ordeal.

20
00:01:49,081 --> 00:01:56,080
The technological advances and comforts integrated into modern vehicles may only mask the dangers.

21
00:01:56,081 --> 00:02:05,080
The trouble with most vehicles they manufacture nowadays is they're more comfortable than sitting in your chair in your front room.

22
00:02:05,081 --> 00:02:14,080
They've got heated seats, they've got cruise control, they've got all the comforts of video and stereo equipment.

23
00:02:14,081 --> 00:02:20,400
equipment and what that does is distract people from what's happening to the road surface

24
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:26,080
when that happens cars can crash or go over the side of the road

25
00:02:27,600 --> 00:02:32,880
caltrans short for the california department of transportation keeps a watchful eye and offers a

26
00:02:32,881 --> 00:02:39,360
helping hand 10-4 yeah we got someone coming to help you out okay once we get these chains on we

27
00:02:39,361 --> 00:02:40,720
We should be all right just to pull it out.

28
00:02:40,721 --> 00:02:41,560
Okay, man.

29
00:02:41,561 --> 00:02:42,400
Thanks.

30
00:02:43,440 --> 00:02:44,460
On this mountain road,

31
00:02:44,461 --> 00:02:47,140
conditions can quickly go from bad to worse.

32
00:02:48,900 --> 00:02:49,900
You get ice,

33
00:02:51,200 --> 00:02:54,000
lots of snow, whiteouts where you can't see,

34
00:02:54,001 --> 00:02:57,420
traffic that assumes they can do whatever they'd like.

35
00:02:58,440 --> 00:03:00,180
It gets to be real difficult out here,

36
00:03:00,181 --> 00:03:02,820
especially in heavy traffic when a good storm's going.

37
00:03:05,680 --> 00:03:06,680
Hey, buddy.

38
00:03:06,681 --> 00:03:07,780
Here.

39
00:03:07,781 --> 00:03:08,840
This is what you're going to want to do.

40
00:03:09,280 --> 00:03:10,540
Throw this over like so.

41
00:03:12,520 --> 00:03:14,700
Severe storms also impact the economy.

42
00:03:15,720 --> 00:03:20,080
Local skiing and gambling are heavily dependent on the traffic that flows through I-80.

43
00:03:22,060 --> 00:03:27,520
Every hour the route is closed costs American businesses about a million dollars from lost tourism,

44
00:03:28,140 --> 00:03:32,100
reduced productivity, and the delay of time-sensitive deliveries.

45
00:03:32,101 --> 00:03:35,640
R2 westbound state line deny act.

46
00:03:35,641 --> 00:03:38,640
Truck train requirements are minimum Kingvale clear.

47
00:03:38,641 --> 00:03:42,380
Keeping I-80 open and safe is the mission of Caltrans.

48
00:03:42,381 --> 00:03:47,220
Kingvale Communication Center acts as headquarters.

49
00:03:47,221 --> 00:03:51,720
Ground Zero for collecting information and coordinating the response.

50
00:03:51,721 --> 00:03:59,320
Its direct feed satellite downloads real-time data on the current snowfall and road conditions.

51
00:03:59,321 --> 00:04:05,320
This is our CHIRP system, our California Highway Internet Reporting Program.

52
00:04:05,321 --> 00:04:11,320
What that does is that it gives real-time Internet access to the public as to what's going on on Highway 80

53
00:04:11,321 --> 00:04:14,320
regarding the chain control from point A to point B.

54
00:04:14,321 --> 00:04:23,320
At the first sign of a storm, trucks with computer-controlled signs are dispatched to key intersections to further update drivers.

55
00:04:23,321 --> 00:04:28,320
This is a TMT truck, which stands for Traffic Management Truck.

56
00:04:28,321 --> 00:04:40,172
It has a computerized sign that notifies the public of how we program it, what the road conditions are, emergencies ahead, slippery roads, accidents.

57
00:04:40,173 --> 00:04:42,320
So it's a good safety tool.

58
00:04:42,321 --> 00:04:45,320
King Vale 72-0.

59
00:04:45,321 --> 00:04:51,320
Continually get traffic counts throughout the area to find out what type of volume of traffic

60
00:04:51,321 --> 00:04:56,320
we're going to have, what to expect if we have an accident or we're holding traffic,

61
00:04:56,321 --> 00:05:00,320
so you're always knowing what your traffic volume is at all times.

62
00:05:03,320 --> 00:05:08,320
King Vale is one of four Caltrans mini-cities of mechanics, technicians, drivers,

63
00:05:08,321 --> 00:05:12,020
drivers, and support personnel that keeps I-80 humming.

64
00:05:13,100 --> 00:05:15,980
We have 40 pieces of snow removal equipment,

65
00:05:16,480 --> 00:05:21,480
and with the support people, mechanics, dispatchers, cooks, supervision,

66
00:05:21,960 --> 00:05:25,520
in a major storm we'll have 80 to 90 people working out of this one station.

67
00:05:27,280 --> 00:05:31,540
Clearing the highway as quickly as possible is the main goal of the snow removal team.

68
00:05:33,060 --> 00:05:36,220
Snow graders, or snow plows, move out first,

69
00:05:36,221 --> 00:05:38,520
followed by powerful snow blowers.

70
00:05:39,960 --> 00:05:43,980
In the early 1990s, the snowplow fleet was modernized,

71
00:05:44,220 --> 00:05:45,660
dramatically increasing efficiency.

72
00:05:47,440 --> 00:05:50,000
Before, if we plowed with a grater,

73
00:05:50,260 --> 00:05:52,420
we would average maybe 6, 7 miles an hour.

74
00:05:52,840 --> 00:05:57,260
Well, now 15 to 18 miles an hour we can operate these graters.

75
00:05:58,640 --> 00:06:03,220
The largest blower we had up here would handle about 3,000 tons an hour.

76
00:06:03,221 --> 00:06:07,520
We picked up a blower that averages now 5,000 tons an hour.

77
00:06:07,860 --> 00:06:12,340
Those units have revolutionized the way we do business on the summit as far as snow removal.

78
00:06:12,700 --> 00:06:14,920
They flat move the snow out.

79
00:06:16,740 --> 00:06:20,420
The snow plows are equipped with blades made of extra hard carbide.

80
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:24,700
Even so, grinding against asphalt and concrete quickly wears them out.

81
00:06:26,260 --> 00:06:30,240
They can be changed once a day or they can last two or three days

82
00:06:30,241 --> 00:06:33,900
depending on the type of snow we have and the amount the equipment is used.

83
00:06:36,940 --> 00:06:40,820
When stalled vehicles block the road and prevent snowplows from operating,

84
00:06:41,640 --> 00:06:44,460
the mighty H2 tow truck comes to the rescue.

85
00:06:45,920 --> 00:06:48,760
Mainly we use it to push big rigs up the hill.

86
00:06:49,220 --> 00:06:51,800
We've got a 7% grade out here that gets pretty icy.

87
00:06:52,480 --> 00:06:55,520
And when they can't get traction, we'll get behind them and push them.

88
00:06:55,521 --> 00:07:04,480
It can push an enormous weight, like an 80,000-pound FedEx truck struggling to get traction of a slippery, steep grade.

89
00:07:07,740 --> 00:07:10,780
Perhaps the most perilous situation is a whiteout.

90
00:07:12,960 --> 00:07:17,420
But newly developed technology helps drivers to see into the darkness.

91
00:07:17,421 --> 00:07:20,260
It navigates just like an airplane would on instruments.

92
00:07:20,261 --> 00:07:26,060
The operator of the truck operates it using a screen that shows him where he is in the road.

93
00:07:26,300 --> 00:07:33,240
It's also equipped with forward and rear-emitting radar sensors that show any obstructions that might be in the highway.

94
00:07:34,640 --> 00:07:37,520
Drivers stay on track with magnet technology.

95
00:07:38,340 --> 00:07:42,500
A view screen in the truck responds to magnets that have been placed in the center of the highway.

96
00:07:44,300 --> 00:07:49,120
By keeping the red dots between the white lines, the drivers stay centered on the road,

97
00:07:49,121 --> 00:07:51,440
even in zero visibility conditions.

98
00:07:52,620 --> 00:07:55,700
There are some areas that are real scary in the whiteout

99
00:07:55,701 --> 00:07:59,480
because they're banked curves and steep downgrades.

100
00:08:00,280 --> 00:08:02,340
But it's more exciting than it is scary

101
00:08:02,341 --> 00:08:06,240
because you're in this big truck, you've got radio contact,

102
00:08:06,860 --> 00:08:08,880
you're talking to the people in front of you,

103
00:08:09,160 --> 00:08:11,540
they're letting you know if there's any cars in the bank,

104
00:08:12,080 --> 00:08:14,180
anybody on foot you have to watch out for.

105
00:08:15,060 --> 00:08:17,100
And it's exciting, it's fun.

106
00:08:17,101 --> 00:08:18,551
It wasn't always fun.

107
00:08:18,552 --> 00:08:27,600
The pioneers who gave the name and their lives to this mountain pass had only the crudest means of overcoming the forces of nature.

108
00:08:28,940 --> 00:08:36,280
In the mid-1800s, countless wagon trains made their way west to California through this narrow passage in the Sierras.

109
00:08:36,281 --> 00:08:45,520
This huge set of mountains that are covered with outcrops of granite have high and low points.

110
00:08:46,220 --> 00:08:50,420
And when immigrants would want to come over, they would look for some lower points.

111
00:08:50,540 --> 00:08:54,060
This is one of the lower points in the middle of this huge range.

112
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:59,620
But at over 7,000 feet elevation, low is a relative term.

113
00:09:00,300 --> 00:09:07,860
On October 31, 1846, the 87 men, women, and children of the Donner Party reached the base of the summit.

114
00:09:08,800 --> 00:09:13,840
They'd begun their trek from the Midwest late in the season and arrived one day too late.

115
00:09:14,720 --> 00:09:18,880
That night, a massive snowfall blocked the passage with drifts 20 feet high.

116
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:27,360
They sent a few men out over the mountains trying to get down to Sutter's Fort and other locations and come back with supplies.

117
00:09:27,361 --> 00:09:31,760
But they weren't going to get the whole group out until the next spring.

118
00:09:31,900 --> 00:09:34,640
By that time, almost half the party had died.

119
00:09:35,460 --> 00:09:37,920
The ordeal would claim 34 lives.

120
00:09:39,280 --> 00:09:43,480
Some survivors warded off starvation by resorting to cannibalism.

121
00:09:44,580 --> 00:09:48,580
The case of the Donner Party exemplifies the importance of mountain roads.

122
00:09:48,740 --> 00:09:50,680
If there had been a road through there at the time,

123
00:09:51,180 --> 00:09:52,920
and if they'd exercised a little bit more caution,

124
00:09:53,240 --> 00:09:56,020
they might have gotten through successfully.

125
00:09:57,360 --> 00:10:01,400
But not even tragedy could diminish the importance of the newly named Donner Pass,

126
00:10:01,860 --> 00:10:05,000
which was to play a still more significant role in Western history.

127
00:10:06,560 --> 00:10:08,520
Three years after the Donner tragedy,

128
00:10:09,300 --> 00:10:12,260
Wells Fargo completed a stagecoach road through the passage.

129
00:10:13,860 --> 00:10:16,860
As you start getting people who are coming out the Oregon Trail

130
00:10:16,861 --> 00:10:19,100
and decided to detour to California,

131
00:10:19,360 --> 00:10:21,080
they're going to try to widen this out.

132
00:10:21,460 --> 00:10:23,000
They're going to be cutting down trees.

133
00:10:23,120 --> 00:10:26,820
They're going to be using the stumps to winch up their wagons.

134
00:10:26,821 --> 00:10:29,200
and this eventually gets wider and wider.

135
00:10:30,320 --> 00:10:35,720
In the 1860s, thousands of Chinese immigrants carved a passageway through the mountains.

136
00:10:37,040 --> 00:10:43,060
Finally in 1869, the first transcontinental railroad connected California with the East Coast.

137
00:10:43,640 --> 00:10:45,440
It moved through Donner Pass.

138
00:10:47,540 --> 00:10:53,140
Early in the 20th century, a route parallel to the rail line was cleared to make way for a new invention,

139
00:10:53,880 --> 00:10:54,960
the automobile.

140
00:10:54,961 --> 00:11:02,720
In 1913, workers completed the Lincoln Highway, a gravel road that was nonetheless the nation's

141
00:11:02,721 --> 00:11:05,600
first transcontinental highway.

142
00:11:05,601 --> 00:11:08,160
Its route was over Donner Pass.

143
00:11:08,161 --> 00:11:13,420
People wanted a route across the country where they could control their own pace and find

144
00:11:13,421 --> 00:11:14,420
their way.

145
00:11:14,421 --> 00:11:15,420
It's kind of an adventure.

146
00:11:15,421 --> 00:11:21,720
In the 1950s, Interstate 80 replaced the Lincoln Highway.

147
00:11:21,721 --> 00:11:23,780
More lanes gave way to eight.

148
00:11:23,781 --> 00:11:25,160
Turns were smoothly banked.

149
00:11:25,161 --> 00:11:30,220
The latest long-lasting mixture of asphalt was laid over the gravel.

150
00:11:30,221 --> 00:11:36,980
The creation of I-80 through Donner Pass exemplifies how road building technology and the administrative

151
00:11:36,981 --> 00:11:41,800
and economic might of the interstate highway system eventually conquered obstacles that

152
00:11:41,801 --> 00:11:44,540
had long challenged American overland travel.

153
00:11:44,541 --> 00:11:49,160
This happened in many places throughout America.

154
00:11:49,161 --> 00:11:53,460
I-70 in Colorado is yet another mountain superhighway.

155
00:11:54,220 --> 00:11:59,980
It's here that the Rockies' rugged terrain would give way to the construction of a monumental tunnel.

156
00:12:02,480 --> 00:12:09,680
The annual cost of Caltrans snow-clearing personnel and equipment in the Donner Pass region exceeds $6 million.

157
00:12:11,100 --> 00:12:14,800
Mountain Roads will return on Modern Marvels.

158
00:12:19,160 --> 00:12:22,400
We now return to Mountain Roads on Modern Marvels.

159
00:12:24,600 --> 00:12:28,040
High in the Rocky Mountains, 60 miles west of Denver,

160
00:12:28,700 --> 00:12:31,740
stands one of the great links in the transportation chain

161
00:12:31,741 --> 00:12:34,020
that connects the regions of the United States.

162
00:12:35,920 --> 00:12:39,140
Eisenhower Tunnel is a four-lane traffic tunnel

163
00:12:39,141 --> 00:12:41,780
at 11,000 feet above sea level.

164
00:12:42,860 --> 00:12:46,760
It transverses the great divide of the Rocky Mountains.

165
00:12:47,320 --> 00:12:48,900
It changed the face of Colorado.

166
00:12:49,160 --> 00:12:56,800
Both commercially and the ski industry have all bloomed since it opened 32 years ago.

167
00:12:58,400 --> 00:13:01,200
Eisenhower is the highest vehicular tunnel in the world,

168
00:13:01,520 --> 00:13:04,580
and it was one of the most difficult to design and construct.

169
00:13:06,100 --> 00:13:08,700
The tunnel was the final link in I-70,

170
00:13:09,380 --> 00:13:12,460
a highway that stretches all the way from Maryland to Utah.

171
00:13:14,340 --> 00:13:17,820
The road was begun on the East Coast during the 1930s

172
00:13:17,821 --> 00:13:21,460
and expanded west to Colorado over the next two decades.

173
00:13:22,460 --> 00:13:27,500
In 1957, workers expanded the road even further west to Utah.

174
00:13:29,420 --> 00:13:32,600
Perhaps the most monumental achievement in I-70's history

175
00:13:32,601 --> 00:13:37,260
was the construction of Colorado's 1.6-mile Eisenhower Tunnel.

176
00:13:40,780 --> 00:13:44,260
Each day, 29,000 vehicles passed through.

177
00:13:44,261 --> 00:13:49,120
Previously, these motorists would have been forced to take the old Loveland Pass,

178
00:13:49,121 --> 00:13:53,900
a route that's a thousand feet higher and adds an hour and a half to each round trip.

179
00:13:55,600 --> 00:14:05,220
The Eisenhower Tunnel, which was designed to bypass a narrow, circuitous, winding, relatively dangerous and hard-to-keep-open roadway,

180
00:14:05,480 --> 00:14:10,080
was a major technological fix to a long-standing problem.

181
00:14:10,081 --> 00:14:17,600
Charles Robinson and Richard Eccles are two of the men responsible for this unique feat of construction.

182
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:20,180
Great fun.

183
00:14:20,760 --> 00:14:21,780
Great fun, uh-huh.

184
00:14:22,200 --> 00:14:24,180
What dirty, nasty old hole in the ground.

185
00:14:24,600 --> 00:14:25,300
Yeah.

186
00:14:26,240 --> 00:14:27,300
Aren't they all?

187
00:14:28,780 --> 00:14:33,240
This area, adjacent to Loveland Pass, was chosen back in the 1960s.

188
00:14:33,820 --> 00:14:36,440
After extensive scouting and geological investigation,

189
00:14:36,441 --> 00:14:44,960
there were going to be many challenges well we knew it was going to be ugly this tunnel

190
00:14:44,961 --> 00:14:52,220
goes through a major what they call a loveland berthed past fault zone or sheer zone and that's

191
00:14:52,221 --> 00:14:58,280
bad rock as a result engineers realized that a single four-lane tunnel was impractical

192
00:14:58,281 --> 00:15:03,980
to reduce the stress they came up with a design featuring a pair of two-lane tunnels

193
00:15:03,981 --> 00:15:10,980
In March of 1968, construction began on the westbound bore.

194
00:15:10,981 --> 00:15:17,540
It would excavate to a height of 48 feet and a width of 40 feet, the largest bore ever

195
00:15:17,541 --> 00:15:20,220
attempted in the United States.

196
00:15:20,221 --> 00:15:28,220
When tunnels are built today, giant custom-made boring machines drill tunnel-sized holes with

197
00:15:28,221 --> 00:15:30,220
relative ease.

198
00:15:30,221 --> 00:15:37,220
But in the 1960s, drilling and blasting was the only way.

199
00:15:37,221 --> 00:15:43,220
Setting charges was a dirty, dangerous, and agonizingly slow process.

200
00:15:43,221 --> 00:15:51,220
They were drilling around 12 to 15 feet, loading it with powder, blowing it out.

201
00:15:51,221 --> 00:15:58,220
Then they would muck all that material out, and then the shield would go ahead again.

202
00:15:58,221 --> 00:16:05,220
After workers blasted and removed a section of rock, large steel supports were positioned around the excavation.

203
00:16:05,221 --> 00:16:08,220
This was known as the shield.

204
00:16:08,221 --> 00:16:17,220
The blue structures there in the model are the arch ribs, the steel arch support ribs that were used to support the mountain as we went through it.

205
00:16:17,221 --> 00:16:22,220
And they were spaced anywhere from three feet to one foot.

206
00:16:22,221 --> 00:16:27,220
In addition, steel reinforcing bars were inserted.

207
00:16:27,221 --> 00:16:34,220
All told, 10,000 tons of steel were used to keep the mountain from collapsing in on itself.

208
00:16:34,221 --> 00:16:42,220
An 800-foot-long stretch of the tunnel ran through a fault, the most unstable area within the mountain.

209
00:16:42,221 --> 00:16:47,220
Engineers and geologists devised a plan to deal with this problem.

210
00:16:47,221 --> 00:16:55,220
They came up with what we call the multi-drift system, a drift being a smaller tunnel that, with steel and concrete,

211
00:16:55,221 --> 00:16:59,340
reinforces what is today's present tunnel.

212
00:17:02,040 --> 00:17:04,200
To support this unstable area,

213
00:17:04,660 --> 00:17:07,100
a series of smaller tunnels, or drifts,

214
00:17:07,340 --> 00:17:09,220
was bored out by drilling and blasting.

215
00:17:10,200 --> 00:17:12,860
Each drift measured approximately 9 feet square

216
00:17:12,861 --> 00:17:14,640
and was filled with concrete.

217
00:17:16,380 --> 00:17:17,920
At the project's peak,

218
00:17:18,440 --> 00:17:21,320
1,140 people labored in three shifts,

219
00:17:21,800 --> 00:17:24,600
24 hours a day, 6 days a week.

220
00:17:25,220 --> 00:17:30,020
The work was exhausting, unending, and sometimes deadly.

221
00:17:30,021 --> 00:17:36,440
When you're underground, it's very dangerous because you're working in a very small, confined area.

222
00:17:36,680 --> 00:17:41,240
You have lots of equipment. It's dark, it's muddy, it's noisy.

223
00:17:41,780 --> 00:17:48,580
Some of the people, too, I think, were backed over by cement trucks, and one fell to his death.

224
00:17:50,440 --> 00:17:54,300
Eventually, 2.5 million cubic yards of rock were excavated.

225
00:17:54,301 --> 00:17:56,900
at a cost of seven lives.

226
00:17:58,040 --> 00:18:02,760
In 1973, three years behind schedule and 100% over budget,

227
00:18:03,340 --> 00:18:05,400
the westbound tunnel was finally completed.

228
00:18:06,240 --> 00:18:10,620
Six years later, in 1979, the eastbound passage followed suit.

229
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:14,420
Total cost? $262 million.

230
00:18:17,260 --> 00:18:20,600
The 10 million cars that pass through the tunnels each year

231
00:18:20,601 --> 00:18:23,640
are exposed to but a small portion of the complex.

232
00:18:24,300 --> 00:18:31,300
Motorists can't see the three cross passages that connect the tunnels at 2,000-foot intervals.

233
00:18:31,301 --> 00:18:39,300
These passages give maintenance workers access to the entire facility and provide for evacuation in case of emergency.

234
00:18:39,301 --> 00:18:51,300
We monitor traffic through 22 monitor zones, 11 in each bore, and we're trying to identify any problems we might have.

235
00:18:51,301 --> 00:18:56,420
It could be a stalled vehicle or, in worst case scenario, a tunnel fire.

236
00:18:56,660 --> 00:18:58,300
What about your overheated vehicle?

237
00:18:59,700 --> 00:19:03,320
In a crisis, Eisenhower can be completely self-sufficient.

238
00:19:04,340 --> 00:19:08,640
The complex maintains its own fire trucks, ready to respond on a moment's notice.

239
00:19:10,100 --> 00:19:13,760
Each tunnel has its own electrical system drawing from the power grid.

240
00:19:14,700 --> 00:19:19,880
Each also has a set of backup generators and banks of batteries in case of a power failure.

241
00:19:21,300 --> 00:19:24,880
This is the local control board for the emergency generator.

242
00:19:25,100 --> 00:19:26,440
It runs on natural gas.

243
00:19:27,140 --> 00:19:33,340
It'll provide our power to tunnel lighting, minimal lighting, building lighting, and minimal ventilation.

244
00:19:35,100 --> 00:19:41,820
As an additional fail-safe, if one tunnel had to be closed, the second could be converted to handle two-way traffic.

245
00:19:43,840 --> 00:19:49,560
One of the biggest concerns with long car tunnels at high altitude is the buildup of carbon monoxide.

246
00:19:51,300 --> 00:19:58,200
The Eisenhower ventilation system is elaborate and frequently adjusted as the density of traffic changes.

247
00:19:59,900 --> 00:20:03,940
Exhaust ducts carrying fresh air run through the tunnels above the ceiling.

248
00:20:04,800 --> 00:20:07,860
We're walking right over the tunnel right now into the north tunnel.

249
00:20:08,860 --> 00:20:10,420
And these are the supply ducts.

250
00:20:11,420 --> 00:20:17,480
And actually the output of the ventilation is behind us.

251
00:20:17,481 --> 00:20:21,320
And what it does is it blows air all the way to the center of the tunnel.

252
00:20:22,700 --> 00:20:26,580
28 giant fans, each more than 10 feet wide,

253
00:20:27,020 --> 00:20:30,820
are capable of moving over a million cubic feet of air every two minutes.

254
00:20:32,220 --> 00:20:36,040
Ventilation stacks tower three stories above the control centers.

255
00:20:37,740 --> 00:20:42,440
Ultimately, they keep everyone, motorists and tunnel workers, breathing safely.

256
00:20:42,441 --> 00:20:49,440
For a quarter of a century, the Eisenhower Tunnel and I-70 have helped motorists get

257
00:20:49,441 --> 00:20:52,940
to their destinations more easily and quickly.

258
00:20:52,941 --> 00:20:57,400
But not all mountain roads are meant for utilitarian purposes.

259
00:20:57,401 --> 00:21:03,380
Montana's going to the Sun Road is a destination unto itself, allowing travelers to go where

260
00:21:03,381 --> 00:21:07,940
few had ever ventured.

261
00:21:07,941 --> 00:21:14,660
During construction of the Eisenhower Tunnel, workers detonated anywhere from 75 to 220 pounds of explosives,

262
00:21:15,280 --> 00:21:19,060
depending on the density of the rock, to gain a four-foot advance.

263
00:21:20,500 --> 00:21:23,440
Mountain Roads will return on Modern Marvels.

264
00:21:25,620 --> 00:21:29,340
We now return to Mountain Roads on Modern Marvels.

265
00:21:29,341 --> 00:21:38,780
Glacier National Park in northwestern Montana is one of the crown jewels of America's park system.

266
00:21:40,140 --> 00:21:45,080
Its 1.2 million acres contains some of the most breathtaking scenery on Earth.

267
00:21:46,420 --> 00:21:51,060
Aside from a few town sites at its borders, this is a land that time forgot.

268
00:21:52,300 --> 00:21:55,720
Human activity is all but invisible among the massive glacial lakes,

269
00:21:55,721 --> 00:22:03,140
windswept alpine tundra and sweeping evergreen forests

270
00:22:03,740 --> 00:22:09,220
there is but one major sign of man in this wilderness the spectacular going to

271
00:22:09,221 --> 00:22:15,200
the Sun Road two narrow lanes that weave for 52 miles through seemingly impassable

272
00:22:15,201 --> 00:22:22,100
terrain it goes from 3,000 feet to the top of Logan Pass which makes you feel

273
00:22:22,101 --> 00:22:23,380
like you're on top of the world.

274
00:22:30,620 --> 00:22:32,160
You kind of look out the window

275
00:22:32,161 --> 00:22:34,360
and you really are awed by the scenery that you see.

276
00:22:34,520 --> 00:22:36,860
It's a beautiful scene that you can drive

277
00:22:36,861 --> 00:22:38,820
in your own personal car and go look at.

278
00:22:40,500 --> 00:22:41,940
Early in the 20th century,

279
00:22:42,100 --> 00:22:44,440
a debate raged within the Department of the Interior

280
00:22:44,441 --> 00:22:47,080
over whether or not to allow roads

281
00:22:47,081 --> 00:22:48,620
in America's national parks.

282
00:22:49,640 --> 00:22:51,460
The final decision was that

283
00:22:51,461 --> 00:22:56,080
these big western parks such as Glacier were going to have probably one major road,

284
00:22:56,320 --> 00:22:59,700
and that major road should also be built to the highest possible standards.

285
00:23:01,100 --> 00:23:07,380
Glacier's Trans Mountain Highway would arguably be the most difficult road ever attempted in American history.

286
00:23:09,000 --> 00:23:16,540
It would traverse dense wilderness and cross the Continental Divide at an elevation of 6,646 feet.

287
00:23:16,541 --> 00:23:24,140
Beginning in September 1924, surveyor Frank Kittredge and his team of 32 men

288
00:23:24,141 --> 00:23:28,780
battled the elements to determine the best possible paths through ice and rock.

289
00:23:29,900 --> 00:23:33,180
They worked in September, October, and November.

290
00:23:33,420 --> 00:23:36,120
There was fog, there was snow, there was sleet, there was hail.

291
00:23:36,500 --> 00:23:39,500
And each day they had to climb higher and higher and higher.

292
00:23:39,501 --> 00:23:44,700
They had to rappel off of cliffs in order to find the survey places

293
00:23:44,701 --> 00:23:46,500
where they wanted to put in the stakes.

294
00:23:48,320 --> 00:23:53,560
Despite the rugged terrain, Kittredge envisioned a road that would nonetheless create a gentle

295
00:23:53,561 --> 00:23:56,820
riding experience, filled with sweeping vistas.

296
00:23:58,260 --> 00:24:02,500
But building his road in the middle of the wilderness with minimal environmental impact

297
00:24:02,501 --> 00:24:04,420
would be a major challenge.

298
00:24:06,340 --> 00:24:11,440
Initially, all of the materials that they needed had to be brought up on horseback.

299
00:24:11,441 --> 00:24:15,080
Whatever equipment they could get up is what they had to use.

300
00:24:15,400 --> 00:24:19,500
You had picks, you had crowbars, and you had shovels that they used.

301
00:24:20,540 --> 00:24:25,240
At first, the decidedly low-tech operation relied on back-breaking labor.

302
00:24:26,220 --> 00:24:32,420
At the peak of construction in the late 1920s, 300 men housed in seven camps cleared the route.

303
00:24:33,080 --> 00:24:34,980
It was like a camping experience.

304
00:24:34,981 --> 00:24:42,620
They were gone up here for the whole season and sometimes into some pretty miserable weather when winter approached.

305
00:24:44,840 --> 00:24:56,060
Workers detonated over 250 tons of explosives to excavate passages with a pound of dynamite for each cubic yard of material blasting.

306
00:24:57,620 --> 00:25:02,160
Setting the charges required a combination of steady nerves and acrobatic daring.

307
00:25:02,860 --> 00:25:04,920
The road is cut right out of the side of a mountain,

308
00:25:05,080 --> 00:25:08,180
and up here in the high country, there are very steep cliffs,

309
00:25:08,280 --> 00:25:11,900
many of them 100 feet, maybe 200, 300 feet sheer cliffs.

310
00:25:12,260 --> 00:25:15,680
And what the road workers had to do is they had to rappel down,

311
00:25:16,180 --> 00:25:18,400
and then they would set their dynamite charges,

312
00:25:18,401 --> 00:25:20,580
and then they would have to get out of the way.

313
00:25:24,460 --> 00:25:29,400
Boulders as large as 2 tons required 6 men with huge crowbars to move them.

314
00:25:29,401 --> 00:25:40,400
Workers laid 24-inch gauge track so that gas locomotives could carry loads of supplies to, and debris from, the ever-moving scene of construction.

315
00:25:44,400 --> 00:25:47,400
But it wasn't just men who built the road.

316
00:25:47,401 --> 00:25:52,400
Forever accessibility permitted, 20th century technology joined them.

317
00:25:52,401 --> 00:25:56,400
That included bulldozers, steam shovels, and cranes.

318
00:25:56,401 --> 00:26:03,120
Nonetheless, stonemasons gave the retaining walls a handcrafted look, building them out

319
00:26:03,121 --> 00:26:07,800
of limestone and argillite.

320
00:26:07,801 --> 00:26:12,700
In 1933, the Going to the Sun Road was completed.

321
00:26:12,701 --> 00:26:16,760
It immediately became a major attraction.

322
00:26:16,761 --> 00:26:21,080
Visitors were thrilled by the views, and local businesses were boosted by an infusion of

323
00:26:21,081 --> 00:26:23,520
tourist dollars.

324
00:26:23,521 --> 00:26:27,920
The road's popularity continues to this day.

325
00:26:27,921 --> 00:26:33,920
But for the National Park Service, the task has now become how to best mix the old with

326
00:26:33,921 --> 00:26:34,920
the new.

327
00:26:34,921 --> 00:26:41,180
There's a challenge in managing mountain roads that are on the one hand historic and

328
00:26:41,181 --> 00:26:43,580
on the other hand fulfilling contemporary uses.

329
00:26:43,581 --> 00:26:50,240
How do we preserve this experience of driving the going to the Sun Road as if you were driving

330
00:26:50,241 --> 00:26:57,240
in 1933 with the pressures that we have today to adapt to contemporary design standards.

331
00:26:57,241 --> 00:27:02,700
A major effort is currently underway to restore and rebuild Glacier's 70-year-old retaining

332
00:27:02,701 --> 00:27:07,420
walls to their original functionality and beauty.

333
00:27:07,421 --> 00:27:12,640
As the project begins on a particular section, workers install steel beams to shore up the

334
00:27:12,641 --> 00:27:14,920
road and prevent collapse.

335
00:27:14,921 --> 00:27:19,920
Then the men can safely reconstruct the retaining walls.

336
00:27:19,921 --> 00:27:27,920
Here you see we have some pretty massive rebar that's been drilled into this historic portion of the road.

337
00:27:27,921 --> 00:27:34,920
Beyond there we have the new concrete core that will get a stone veneer over it

338
00:27:34,921 --> 00:27:39,920
and using native stone make it look like the original construction.

339
00:27:39,921 --> 00:27:45,440
once the roads brought up the grade and all this is filled in now this wall is

340
00:27:45,441 --> 00:27:49,860
footed where originally during the original construction it had no concrete

341
00:27:49,861 --> 00:27:55,120
footing at all the goal of the caretakers of the going to the Sun Road

342
00:27:55,121 --> 00:27:59,720
is to maintain the structural integrity and beauty of this beloved road for

343
00:27:59,721 --> 00:28:05,480
future generations and just as the going to the Sun opened up scenic wonders of

344
00:28:05,481 --> 00:28:06,181
the West.

345
00:28:06,182 --> 00:28:11,420
A few years after its completion, another major mountain road would reveal the

346
00:28:11,421 --> 00:28:13,339
American Southeast.

347
00:28:13,340 --> 00:28:21,320
Going to the Sun Road handled about 40,000 cars in 1934, the year it officially

348
00:28:21,321 --> 00:28:24,869
opened. Today, it carries 500,000 cars yearly.

349
00:28:24,870 --> 00:28:28,340
Mountain Roads will return on Modern Marvels.

350
00:28:29,640 --> 00:28:32,960
We now return to Mountain Roads on Modern Marvels.

351
00:28:35,480 --> 00:28:43,900
The world's largest rural parkway runs for an astonishing 469 miles through one of America's most scenic regions.

352
00:28:45,400 --> 00:28:51,520
The Shenandoah National Park in Virginia and the Great Smoky Mountains National Park in North Carolina and Tennessee

353
00:28:51,521 --> 00:28:57,020
are connected by an intricately landscaped passage, the Blue Ridge Parkway.

354
00:28:58,180 --> 00:29:03,540
Parkways are roads that traverse parks or scenic areas and are largely designed for recreational motoring.

355
00:29:03,541 --> 00:29:08,700
It's not getting there from point A to point B quickly, it is the enjoyment of the drive itself.

356
00:29:09,820 --> 00:29:13,760
This mountain road passes through an ever-changing natural world.

357
00:29:14,440 --> 00:29:19,200
One of the really interesting things about the parkway is the ephemeral nature of the parkway.

358
00:29:19,580 --> 00:29:24,060
The fog today is a great example of that, just as the seasons are as well.

359
00:29:24,540 --> 00:29:29,980
In the spring with the coming out of the trees and the shrubs and the azaleas, all the native plants,

360
00:29:29,981 --> 00:29:35,140
following through the summer with the bright greens into the fall with the wonderful colors that we have.

361
00:29:36,140 --> 00:29:43,700
Since the 1950s, the Blue Ridge Parkway has reigned as the single most visited feature in the entire national park system.

362
00:29:45,300 --> 00:29:51,580
The parkway goes through what appears to be a pristine, undisturbed landscape,

363
00:29:51,880 --> 00:29:57,260
a place where the trappings of our modern lives have not entered.

364
00:29:57,261 --> 00:30:04,260
The Blue Ridge Parkway was intended as much to celebrate the disappearing cultural landscape of the Appalachias

365
00:30:04,261 --> 00:30:11,260
as it was to celebrate the often destroyed and recovering natural landscape of the Appalachias.

366
00:30:12,260 --> 00:30:20,260
In the 1930s, President Franklin Roosevelt backed the proposed road as a way to generate large numbers of public works jobs

367
00:30:20,261 --> 00:30:22,260
for people affected by the Great Depression.

368
00:30:22,261 --> 00:30:23,068
impression.

369
00:30:23,069 --> 00:30:28,500
Roosevelt used funds from the National Industrial Recovery Act to actually

370
00:30:28,501 --> 00:30:34,520
authorize the beginning of the construction, and so on September the 11th, 1935, construction

371
00:30:34,521 --> 00:30:39,500
began on what would ultimately become the longest rural parkway in this country.

372
00:30:39,501 --> 00:30:46,480
While the Federal Civilian Conservation Corps, or CCC, lined up much of the manpower, these

373
00:30:46,481 --> 00:30:51,260
men were actually hired and paid directly by private industry, under the auspices of

374
00:30:51,261 --> 00:30:57,760
the Federal Highway Administration and the National Park Service.

375
00:30:57,761 --> 00:31:00,180
Contractors formed construction gangs.

376
00:31:00,181 --> 00:31:05,500
The road was divided into 40 sections of 10 to 15 miles each.

377
00:31:05,501 --> 00:31:10,460
Work began on several sections at once, with priority given to areas where jobs were most

378
00:31:10,461 --> 00:31:13,740
desperately needed.

379
00:31:13,741 --> 00:31:17,660
Most of the early road clearing and tunnel digging was done with picks and shovels and

380
00:31:17,661 --> 00:31:19,740
saws.

381
00:31:19,741 --> 00:31:23,820
As paths were cleared, machinery aided the effort.

382
00:31:23,821 --> 00:31:27,760
There were dozers, there was heavy equipment, there were dump trucks, and they used all

383
00:31:27,761 --> 00:31:30,920
of that because you couldn't do a project of this size by hand.

384
00:31:30,921 --> 00:31:37,160
The technology was a product of whatever the time was, and that changed over the 50 years

385
00:31:37,161 --> 00:31:39,200
in which the parkway was built.

386
00:31:39,201 --> 00:31:45,940
A young landscape architect named Stanley Abbott was put in charge of the design.

387
00:31:45,941 --> 00:31:51,260
He wanted the entire parkway to showcase beautiful views, and he hiked through many miles of isolated

388
00:31:51,261 --> 00:31:53,620
countryside to choose the best route.

389
00:31:53,621 --> 00:31:57,700
There weren't many roads, certainly, that came through the mountains, and so there was

390
00:31:57,701 --> 00:32:03,780
a lot of field work going from place to place, looking for what might provide promising overlooks

391
00:32:03,781 --> 00:32:06,940
or great views of scenery.

392
00:32:06,941 --> 00:32:12,820
Unlike many earlier roads, the Blue Ridge would provide motorists with comfortable driving

393
00:32:12,821 --> 00:32:14,820
conditions and compelling scenery.

394
00:32:14,821 --> 00:32:23,720
In the Appalachian region, many of the mountain roads went from valleys up the mountainside, up into a community that would have been at a higher elevation.

395
00:32:23,880 --> 00:32:27,220
These roads were frequently very difficult to drive.

396
00:32:27,460 --> 00:32:35,180
The Blue Ridge Parkway, by contrast, goes along the ridgetops through most of its length, especially in North Carolina.

397
00:32:35,181 --> 00:32:39,900
When you had a ridge that ran perpendicular to the main ridge and you were going to have

398
00:32:39,901 --> 00:32:45,260
to either cut through that ridge or go through with the tunnel, it really preserved the landscape

399
00:32:45,261 --> 00:32:50,140
so that you didn't have these massive cuts and fills.

400
00:32:50,141 --> 00:32:55,940
The Parkway's 26 tunnels were created using the technology of the day, drilling bore holes

401
00:32:55,941 --> 00:33:03,360
and then blasting through rock with dynamite.

402
00:33:03,361 --> 00:33:08,260
of the nearly 170 bridges were built utilizing an arch design using natural

403
00:33:08,261 --> 00:33:13,500
stone excavated from the area. Bridges were used so that there wouldn't be so

404
00:33:13,501 --> 00:33:17,220
much cut and fill in places where you really wanted to preserve the

405
00:33:17,221 --> 00:33:23,480
environment. Designers and builders aimed for a natural handcrafted look.

406
00:33:23,481 --> 00:33:28,240
Structures along the route were finished with facings of local stone, giving the

407
00:33:28,241 --> 00:33:31,800
appearance that they dated from a pre-concrete era.

408
00:33:31,801 --> 00:33:36,440
A lot of that stonework was done by sort of old world artisans, stone masons,

409
00:33:36,600 --> 00:33:39,280
who were brought over from Italy and from Spain.

410
00:33:39,840 --> 00:33:43,960
And so a lot of that work was done by people who were very highly skilled.

411
00:33:45,520 --> 00:33:48,920
World War II interrupted construction on the Blue Ridge Parkway.

412
00:33:50,020 --> 00:33:53,180
After the war, progress resumed slowly, yet steadily.

413
00:33:54,900 --> 00:34:00,620
By 1968, only one seven-mile link at Grandfather Mountain was still missing.

414
00:34:01,800 --> 00:34:06,240
But North Carolina's grandfather, Mountain, was owned by a man named Hugh Morton.

415
00:34:07,220 --> 00:34:13,980
Smart, determined, and wealthy, Morton fought the government for decades over the environmental impact the road would have on his mountain.

416
00:34:15,620 --> 00:34:18,980
It appeared that Morton and the government were at a permanent impasse.

417
00:34:19,900 --> 00:34:26,820
Then, in the 1980s, the conflict was resolved by an engineering marvel, the Lynn Cove Viaduct.

418
00:34:27,820 --> 00:34:31,940
What's amazing about the Lynn Cove Viaduct is how it actually appears to leave the mountain,

419
00:34:32,160 --> 00:34:35,060
float around the boulders, and then come back into the mountain.

420
00:34:35,500 --> 00:34:38,220
And that was the whole idea behind the Lynn Cove Viaduct,

421
00:34:38,300 --> 00:34:43,420
is how do you build a road around a boulder field as unique as this boulder field is?

422
00:34:44,120 --> 00:34:47,420
All viaducts carry roads over and above something.

423
00:34:48,780 --> 00:34:51,900
Designed with software that had only recently been devised,

424
00:34:52,740 --> 00:34:55,980
Lynn Cove is an elaborate double S-curve elevated bridge

425
00:34:55,981 --> 00:34:59,280
that skirts the side of the mountain rather than cutting into it.

426
00:35:01,060 --> 00:35:05,840
The viaduct was built using a so-called progressive segmental construction technique.

427
00:35:06,840 --> 00:35:09,020
This was a first for an American bridge.

428
00:35:10,520 --> 00:35:13,120
Abutments were built to tie the bridge into the landscape.

429
00:35:14,280 --> 00:35:17,300
Then, segments of the bridge were added one by one,

430
00:35:17,880 --> 00:35:21,300
each cantilevered out, until they reached the next support pier.

431
00:35:21,900 --> 00:35:28,460
The segments were epoxied together and then secured by wires that were tightened after the epoxy had hardened.

432
00:35:30,000 --> 00:35:35,020
Eventually, 153 segments joined to complete the 1,300-foot long span.

433
00:35:37,040 --> 00:35:46,520
The Linkov viaduct is clearly one of the most spectacular engineering accomplishments of the last quarter of the 20th century.

434
00:35:46,521 --> 00:35:58,711
Yet the entire reason to employ this complex technology and sophisticated and expensive construction process was to construct a roadway that would lie lightly on the

435
00:35:58,712 --> 00:35:59,412
land.

436
00:35:59,700 --> 00:36:06,220
In 1987, 52 years after construction had begun, the Blue Ridge Parkway was completed.

437
00:36:07,600 --> 00:36:15,340
To the millions of motorists who have traveled the parkway over the years, its serenity has provided relief from the fast pace of modern life.

438
00:36:16,520 --> 00:36:22,920
But on one mountain road, top speed is everything.

439
00:36:23,680 --> 00:36:26,960
It's a testing ground where the winner becomes king of the mountain.

440
00:36:28,620 --> 00:36:31,540
To give motorists plenty of sightseeing opportunities,

441
00:36:32,200 --> 00:36:37,160
the Blue Ridge Parkway was constructed with more than 250 overlooks and parking areas.

442
00:36:38,700 --> 00:36:41,660
Mountain Roads will return on Modern Marvels.

443
00:36:41,661 --> 00:36:48,080
We now return to Mountain Roads on Modern Marvels.

444
00:36:50,980 --> 00:36:58,600
For the most part, mountain roads afford motorists an opportunity to observe the interplay between natural and man-made wonders.

445
00:36:59,780 --> 00:37:05,380
For motorists traveling up to Pikes Peak, outside of Colorado Springs, Colorado, this is certainly true.

446
00:37:06,780 --> 00:37:09,580
You don't have to be a mountain climber. You don't have to even be in shape.

447
00:37:10,240 --> 00:37:13,880
You can get in your car and you can get to over 14,000 feet.

448
00:37:16,440 --> 00:37:20,660
More precisely, at an elevation of 14,110 feet,

449
00:37:21,040 --> 00:37:23,980
this is the highest summit road anywhere in the United States.

450
00:37:26,240 --> 00:37:31,520
But over the last century, the road up to Pikes Peak has also inspired the dreams of speed demons

451
00:37:31,521 --> 00:37:36,060
from all over the world, as the road has become America's highest test track.

452
00:37:36,061 --> 00:37:45,060
Every summer since 1916, this peaceful land has given way to the roar of motorcycles,

453
00:37:45,061 --> 00:37:51,060
the rumble of cars, the screech of diesel trucks,

454
00:37:51,061 --> 00:37:56,060
vehicles of every type competing in the race to the clouds.

455
00:37:56,061 --> 00:38:02,060
The world's finest drivers come to test themselves and their vehicles at pipe speed.

456
00:38:06,060 --> 00:38:08,960
The first winners came in with times of around 20 minutes.

457
00:38:10,020 --> 00:38:12,780
As car technology improved, so did the records.

458
00:38:13,800 --> 00:38:15,260
These days, they're cut in half.

459
00:38:16,320 --> 00:38:19,980
New Zealander Rod Millen drove a souped-up Toyota Celica to the top

460
00:38:19,981 --> 00:38:23,760
and achieved record speeds several times during the 1990s.

461
00:38:24,500 --> 00:38:27,780
His best effort was a time of 10 minutes and 4 seconds.

462
00:38:28,400 --> 00:38:32,700
This is a race against the clock, but it's also a race against the mountain,

463
00:38:32,960 --> 00:38:35,240
and to win, you have to get to the top of the mountain.

464
00:38:35,241 --> 00:38:40,400
And this place will throw weather at you, it'll throw changes in the character of the road.

465
00:38:41,740 --> 00:38:43,280
The mountain's got its own mystique.

466
00:38:45,140 --> 00:38:51,320
At speeds that can exceed 120 miles per hour, around blind curves and mostly on loose gravel,

467
00:38:52,020 --> 00:38:56,920
each driver has the road to himself as he aims for the fastest time against all comers.

468
00:38:58,500 --> 00:39:03,700
The course's 12-plus miles feature a stomach-turning 156 turns.

469
00:39:05,240 --> 00:39:13,400
The mountain steep pitch, mostly gravel surface, and high elevation all present problems that have led to automotive innovations.

470
00:39:15,340 --> 00:39:22,260
From 1954 to 2004, General Motors operated the Pikes Peak Vehicle Test Headquarters.

471
00:39:23,840 --> 00:39:29,660
One of the big problems that we have on Pikes Peak is fluids that boil at 14,000 feet.

472
00:39:29,661 --> 00:39:34,480
As an example, your brake fluid that you would have in your everyday car 10 years ago,

473
00:39:34,660 --> 00:39:37,500
when you got to the top of Pikes Peak, would literally be boiling.

474
00:39:38,240 --> 00:39:43,320
And so they developed new technologies and lubricants and brake fluids and transmission fluids

475
00:39:43,321 --> 00:39:46,560
that makes it safer for the everyday public and their personal vehicles.

476
00:39:47,000 --> 00:39:52,420
Because of the high altitude, unrelenting pressure can cause an engine's combustion chamber to malfunction.

477
00:39:53,840 --> 00:39:59,140
That's why Shell Oil tested its special Rotella T-Oil formula here on a Freightliner truck.

478
00:39:59,660 --> 00:40:06,660
Over the course of the many years of the race, other automotive innovations have been made.

479
00:40:06,661 --> 00:40:11,660
These have included the development of better tires and better suspensions.

480
00:40:11,661 --> 00:40:19,660
Manufacturers have tested all-wheel drive, fuel injection, turbocharging, and even alternative fuel systems on the mountain.

481
00:40:19,661 --> 00:40:25,660
Long before car manufacturers and race car drivers were trying to make a name for themselves here,

482
00:40:25,661 --> 00:40:31,480
themselves here, an explorer, Lieutenant Zebulon Pike, earned the distinction of having the

483
00:40:31,481 --> 00:40:33,224
mountain named after him.

484
00:40:33,225 --> 00:40:37,620
In 1806, he led an expedition surveying the farthest reaches of

485
00:40:37,621 --> 00:40:40,896
the Louisiana Purchase, using the mountain as a guide.

486
00:40:40,897 --> 00:40:43,020
It soon became a national landmark.

487
00:40:44,640 --> 00:40:48,129
It was the identifying feature for Colorado.

488
00:40:48,130 --> 00:40:51,300
And so nationally, even internationally,

489
00:40:51,301 --> 00:40:54,480
the entire state of Colorado really was known as the Pike's Peak region.

490
00:40:55,660 --> 00:41:04,500
In 1889, in order to take tourists to the summit, workers built a carriage road using picks and shovels and mules and wagons.

491
00:41:05,900 --> 00:41:11,180
In 1891, a nine-mile-long steam-powered cog railroad was added.

492
00:41:11,900 --> 00:41:18,400
Before the Pikes Peak Highway, you had the cog, and people had to pay dearly to take that ride.

493
00:41:18,580 --> 00:41:21,400
Or you could ride on a mule train or hike yourself.

494
00:41:21,401 --> 00:41:28,500
Then in the early 1900s, Spencer Penrose, a wealthy mining entrepreneur, decided to

495
00:41:28,501 --> 00:41:34,280
develop the area for tourism with a grand European-style hotel and an exhilarating ride

496
00:41:34,281 --> 00:41:36,280
up to the top of Pikes Peak.

497
00:41:36,281 --> 00:41:39,360
A new road would have to be built.

498
00:41:39,361 --> 00:41:45,480
The construction at that time in 1915, 1916 would have been largely a result of draft

499
00:41:45,481 --> 00:41:48,160
animals, horses, and manpower.

500
00:41:48,161 --> 00:41:52,480
Very few machines were involved in the construction of the road.

501
00:41:52,481 --> 00:41:57,480
Perhaps some drills and dynamiting, but really it was hard physical labor.

502
00:41:57,481 --> 00:42:01,920
The road was completed on August 1, 1916.

503
00:42:01,921 --> 00:42:04,920
It was an immediate success.

504
00:42:04,921 --> 00:42:10,560
To drive a road that went nowhere except up and down the mountain, tourists were willing

505
00:42:10,561 --> 00:42:16,660
to pay what was then the exorbitant toll of $2.50 per car.

506
00:42:16,661 --> 00:42:19,060
and Penrose came up with an even better idea.

507
00:42:19,061 --> 00:42:22,040
1916, he wanted to advertise the highway

508
00:42:22,041 --> 00:42:24,660
by having a race on it, and then in turn,

509
00:42:24,661 --> 00:42:26,900
that gives something for people to do,

510
00:42:26,901 --> 00:42:29,100
and then hopefully they'll come stay in this hotel.

511
00:42:31,200 --> 00:42:33,540
Today, over 300,000 vehicles

512
00:42:33,541 --> 00:42:36,320
travel the Pikes Peak Road annually.

513
00:42:36,321 --> 00:42:38,240
But the gravel from the road is running off

514
00:42:38,241 --> 00:42:40,560
and accumulating in gullies and streams.

515
00:42:41,780 --> 00:42:44,120
This process, called sedimentation,

516
00:42:44,121 --> 00:42:46,620
is choking surrounding trees and marshes.

517
00:42:46,621 --> 00:42:53,340
To deal with this problem, the city of Colorado Springs is paving the entire roadway, a near

518
00:42:53,341 --> 00:42:56,600
decade-long project due for completion in 2010.

519
00:42:56,601 --> 00:43:01,440
We're now standing at the spot where we started construction in 2001.

520
00:43:01,441 --> 00:43:06,100
I'm on the shoulder of the road, and as you can see, this is an example of the Pikes Peak

521
00:43:06,101 --> 00:43:07,100
gravel.

522
00:43:07,101 --> 00:43:13,020
It's decomposed, it's weathered, it's breaking down, and it's, it used to be what we would

523
00:43:13,021 --> 00:43:16,293
called Pikes Peak granite, natural to the mountain.

524
00:43:16,294 --> 00:43:18,540
And what we've done now is actually

525
00:43:18,541 --> 00:43:20,311
covered it with the asphalt surface.

526
00:43:20,312 --> 00:43:22,820
So we've hardened the surface, we've stabilized the

527
00:43:22,821 --> 00:43:29,220
surface, and we're going to prevent that runoff that would generate the sedimentation.

528
00:43:29,221 --> 00:43:33,480
Some fear that a paved road will obscure the area's history and change the unique character

529
00:43:33,481 --> 00:43:36,700
of the race. Others are reserving judgment.

530
00:43:36,701 --> 00:43:42,880
I suppose we'll have to wait and see how history judges the change to the road. But

531
00:43:42,881 --> 00:43:45,820
They've been adding different types of vehicles that go up the mountain.

532
00:43:46,420 --> 00:43:51,080
And this should add a new perspective to the challenge of the Pikes Peak Hill Climb.

533
00:43:52,300 --> 00:43:55,000
Mountain roads have always been about change.

534
00:43:55,600 --> 00:43:59,220
For the past century, they have drastically altered the American landscape

535
00:43:59,221 --> 00:44:02,460
and opened great wildernesses to anyone with a car.

536
00:44:03,980 --> 00:44:09,460
These mountain roads enable us to celebrate much of what we feel is grand and good about America.

537
00:44:09,461 --> 00:44:13,360
our technological expertise, and at the same time,

538
00:44:13,361 --> 00:44:17,060
our ability to meld technological expertise with a reverence for nature,

539
00:44:17,061 --> 00:44:20,260
which we have long prided ourselves in as Americans.

540
00:44:20,261 --> 00:44:24,260
The many builders of America's mountain roads

541
00:44:24,261 --> 00:44:26,760
have entered areas previously closed to man.

542
00:44:26,761 --> 00:44:30,860
Their challenge has been to maintain the delicate balance

543
00:44:30,861 --> 00:44:33,460
between serving and controlling nature,

544
00:44:33,461 --> 00:44:35,660
and time will be the ultimate judge

545
00:44:35,661 --> 00:44:38,260
of how well they've accomplished their goals.

546
00:44:39,460 --> 00:44:44,420
From the smallest doorways to the busiest

